  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 AM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chilly and dry conditions start the week. \n\nEarly morning analysis shows a positively tilted mid-level trough \nand sfc high pressure ~1033 mb centered over the Ohio River Valley. \nMostly clear skies are seen via satellite, except for Bay streamers \nproviding cloud cover over coastal SE VA and NE NC. Low temperatures \nthis morning are a few degrees warmer than yesterday, with most \ninland areas seeing low to mid 30s and upper 30s along the coast. \nTemperatures today will be similar to yesterday, in the upper 50s, \nbut there will be more sunshine and less wind, making it feel a \nlittle warmer than yesterday. Tonight into Tuesday morning will have \na good set up for radiational cooling, as high pressure will be \ndirectly overhead providing little to no winds and clear skies. As a \nresult, lows will be in the upper 20s to low 30s inland and mid 30s \nright along the coast. Tuesday will be sunny with highs around\n60F. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Temperatures show a gradual warming trend mid-\nweek through the weekend. Remaining dry. \n\nHigh pressure remains largely in place through the end of the week. \nAloft, the tilted trough looks to finally move offshore around mid \nweek, with a ridge building in behind it. This will allow for high \ntemperatures to gradually increase each day through Friday, \nremaining moderate through the weekend. Forecast highs are in the \nmid 60s for Wednesday, then upper 60s to near 70 F Thurs-Sat. Lows \nwill likely still drop into the 30s each morning. This pattern will \nbe sunshine and dry weather, with no relief from the dry spell on \nthe horizon.",
